# Future directions

`Catalog/Logic/ProofSpaceTransitionWindows.lean` advances the finite transition model from pointwise descent to blockwise drift. It proves that strict decrease only at sampled block endpoints still yields a unique first sampled crossing, permanent negativity at later sampled endpoints, and localization of the crossing to the block between the last positive endpoint and the first nonpositive endpoint.

The file also connects exact-length shells to cumulative counts. The change in cumulative imbalance is exactly the newly added shell imbalance, so strict cumulative descent is equivalent to an unresolved majority in each new shell. A uniform shell deficit gives linear cumulative decay. Corresponding sampled linear-decay and threshold-location bounds are proved for arbitrary block widths.

No computational evidence is needed for these abstract universal statements: no concrete formula grammar, proof calculus, encoding, or sequence of counts is specified. Numerical data under such circumstances would reflect an arbitrary choice of model rather than test the theorem. The results are symbolic consequences of their explicit finite hypotheses.

Natural next targets are:

1. weaken strict endpoint descent to a quantitative negative drift over variable-length blocks;
2. bound transition-window width when only average drift and bounded positive excursions are assumed;
3. instantiate shell counts using a finite grammar and certified bounded proof checker;
4. compare windows under length recodings with additive or multiplicative distortion bounds; and
5. formulate probabilistic shell deficits and derive high-probability crossing-window bounds.
